{"reference":"Daniel 1","book":"Daniel","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Many scholars distinguish the chapter’s sixth-century narrative setting from the dates they propose for the book’s tales, visions, collection, and final form. On this view, Daniel’s received form brings together traditions and literary stages associated with Persian and Hellenistic periods, reaching final form during the Antiochene crisis. This remains a disputed scholarly account, so the chapter’s setting should not be treated as automatically settling questions about the book’s composition.","verse_refs":["Daniel 1:1-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_daniel_001_2be083cf49df520348877e98"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"Several cautions keep this story from being turned into a simple formula. The opening reference to Jehoiakim’s third year should not be casually equated with the securely attested 597 BCE deportation or Jerusalem’s destruction in 587/586 BCE. Likewise, the chapter depicts a negotiated ten-day food test; it does not say that every royal food item was non-kosher or establish a timeless “Daniel diet.” Babylonian education and new names are part of the story’s pressure on these Judean captives, but the text does not make every cross-cultural education, language, or adopted name an act of apostasy.","verse_refs":["Daniel 1:1-21","Daniel 1:1-7","Daniel 1:3-21","Daniel 1:8-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_daniel_001_f46bd060e29fc858cdef79de"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}